# Decoupled Pagination: Managing Multiple Data Sets on One Page

When you are building a dashboard or an admin panel, it is common to display multiple data grids side-by-side. You might have one table listing recent orders and another showing active users. When you implement Laravel's built-in pagination for the first table using `appends(['page' => $paginator->currentPage()])`, the URL becomes `/dashboard?page=2`.

The immediate problem arises when that user navigates to page 2 of the orders. The browser updates the URL, but your application interprets the `page` query parameter globally. If your second table also listens for a `page` parameter to determine which slice of data to fetch, both grids jump to their second page simultaneously. This creates a disjointed user experience where actions on one list unexpectedly affect another.

To solve this, you need to decouple the pagination state of each independent data source. The goal is to achieve a URL structure like `/dashboard?orders_page=2&users_page=1`, allowing each table to maintain its own scroll position and data slice without interfering with the other.

## Implementing Custom Query Parameters

The most straightforward approach involves overriding how Laravel handles pagination parameters for specific controllers or views. You can define unique query string keys for each paginated resource.

For your orders table, instead of appending a generic `page` parameter, you append `orders_page`. Similarly, for the users table, you use `users_page`. This requires modifying the `paginate()` calls in your controller logic.

```php
// Controller Logic for Orders
$orders = Order::query()
    ->with('customer')
    ->paginate(15, ['*'], 'orders_page');

return view('dashboard.orders', [
    'orders' => $orders,
    // Pass a custom key to the view so it appends the correct param
    'paginationKey' => 'orders_page' 
]);

// Controller Logic for Users
$users = User::query()
    ->paginate(15, ['*'], 'users_page');

return view('dashboard.users', [
    'users' => $users,
    'paginationKey' => 'users_page'
]);
```

In your Blade templates, you must ensure the pagination links explicitly use these custom keys. Laravel's default `{{ $links }}` helper will generate the correct URL based on the paginator instance, but you need to verify that the `page` parameter being appended matches the name you defined in the query builder.

If you are using a single view to render both tables, you can pass distinct paginators and their specific keys to the view:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/dashboard/index.blade.php --}}
<div class="dashboard-container">

    {{-- Orders Table --}}
    <div class="table-wrapper orders">
        <h3>Orders</h3>
        @include('partials.orders-table', ['data' => $orders])

        {{-- Ensure the paginator uses the custom key --}}
        {{ $orders->links()->setPageName('orders_page') }}
    </div>

    {{-- Users Table --}}
    <div class="table-wrapper users">
        <h3>Users</h3>
        @include('partials.users-table', ['data' => $users])

        {{ $users->links()->setPageName('users_page') }}
    </div>

</div>
```

Note the use of `setPageName()`. By default, Laravel appends `page` to the URL. You must explicitly tell the paginator instance which parameter name it should append so that the generated URLs reflect your custom keys. This ensures that clicking a link for page 2 of orders only updates `orders_page`, leaving `users_page` untouched.

## Handling State in the Request

Once you have decoupled the parameters, managing the state becomes slightly more explicit but significantly cleaner. When fetching data in subsequent requests, you must read from these specific keys rather than a generic `page` attribute.

```php
// Inside your controller method
$request = request();

$ordersPage = $request->get('orders_page', 1);
$usersPage = $request->get('users_page', 1);

$orders = Order::query()->paginate(15, ['*'], 'orders_page');
$users = User::query()->paginate(15, ['*'], 'users_page');
```

By using `request()->get('key')`, you ensure that if the user refreshes the page while on page 2 of orders and page 1 of users, both positions are preserved independently. If a specific table has no query parameter set, falling back to `1` ensures the pagination logic doesn't break due to missing input.

## Trade-offs and Considerations

While this approach solves the immediate conflict, it introduces complexity in link generation. You lose the ability to use generic helpers that assume a single pagination context for the entire page. Every paginated resource on the page must be treated as an isolated entity with its own state management.

If you are building a highly complex dashboard where users frequently switch between many filtered lists, consider whether this level of granularity is necessary. Sometimes, consolidating data into a single query or using a separate route per table might be more maintainable if the UI complexity grows too high.

However, for standard dashboards requiring two distinct grids on one page, decoupling via custom query parameters is the robust solution. It keeps your URL state clean and predictable, ensuring that navigation within one dataset never triggers unintended jumps in another.
